Optionally ignore a binary window whose two rows both carry a signal (Mpp2ButtonPurity)

An MPP v2 pen sends the state of its barrel button as a tone in one of two rows of the first
binary window of a group, and iptsd takes row 5 stronger than row 4 for "button or eraser". Whether
it is the button or the eraser is then read from the phase of the tone in the button window.

Measured on the Surface Pro 9 with the Slim Pen 2: the stronger of the two rows normally has more
than 1000 times the magnitude of the other one (95 % of the frames; less while the pen hovers
high). For two to three frames while the pen comes back into range, or changes what it
sends in the second binary window, both rows carry a signal in both windows: the stronger one 3 to
17 times the weaker one in the first window, 1 to 14 times in the second. In those frames the
first window read as "button or eraser", and with no tone at all in the button window (magnitude
5 to 30, where a decision by magnitude needs 1000) the phase of the noise made it a pressed
button: three frames in one capture (45 ms, beginning 75 ms after the pen came into range) and two
in another, with nobody touching the button.

With Mpp2ButtonPurity a binary window decides only if its stronger row has at least that many
times the magnitude of the weaker one. A first window that fails keeps the decision of the frame
before; a second window that fails takes back what the first one of its group decided. With 40
the five frames are gone and no other sample of 7 captures changes.

There was no capture with the button really pressed. A pressed button is expected to be a clean
tone in row 5 like the released one is in row 4, and a single mixed frame while it is pressed or
released keeps the state of the frame before.

Off by default.

